{"kind":"assert","text":"STOP under ruling 28295c20 point 3, for the cutover head (05d618e2). Counted in full, the birth bytes push a byte cap past a definition that is admitted today.\n\nI measured with the existing fixture at PROPOSED_BOUNDS, where entryBytes is 262,144 and usesPerEntry is 128, at WIP a31fdb6d. `certifiedWrittenPlanBudgetV17` in packages/platform/src/destination-budget-v17.ts forecasts the platform destination's written plan. It now charges 2 x 128 uses, one birth per later use:\n- read-publish: 256,474 today, 304,730 with births.\n- read: 254,657 today, 302,913 with births.\n- evidence-plan: 129,687 today, 177,943 with births.\n- push: 51,542 today, 99,798 with births.\n- receipt: 50,142 today, 98,398 with births.\n\nThe read-publish and read variants pass entryBytes, so `admitted` turns false. Five tests in evidence-funding-preview-v17.test.ts then fail with `evidence-funding-unavailable`. In effect, destination@17 loses its evidence path. The other changes cause no failure: the 8-use frame, the 10-use outcome forecast and the evidence-forecast change. No pinned v17 vector or digest changes.\n\nChoose one:\n(a) Bound this forecast's birth uses by the foreign sequences that the written plan can name, not by usesPerEntry. Every added birth belongs to a distinct foreign sequence, so births \u003c= min(later uses, sequences the plan can name). This keeps point 3, because the cap still charges every birth that can occur. It needs the plan's sequence bound. I state it from source in the note.\n(b) Keep the v17 forecast arithmetic as it is, and charge births from the next forecast version. The runtime byte cap still refuses an oversized entry, so v17 forecasts under its real worst case.\n(c) Accept that destination@17 is not admitted, and replace it in a later head.\n\nI lean to (a), if the source gives a small bound on the foreign sequences in one written-plan entry. (a) keeps the forecast truthful and raises no cap. If that bound is near 128, (a) fails the same way, and I take (b).\n\nTwo other facts:\n1. The creation.test.ts fault (RPC receiver lacks \"apply\") comes from the cutover branch. At main 2216316e, \"entry 0 holds the seed\" passes in 5.5 s. On the branch it fails in 8 ms, then hangs. The lane is diagnosing it.\n2. Five v17 tests failed before this change, at WIP de0d44ba: 1 in destination-budget and 4 in evidence-capacity-v17. They come from earlier cutover work, and the lane is diagnosing them.\n\ntsc reports 0 errors over bytes, derive, platform and scope at a31fdb6d."}